  "preamble": "The voiced alveolar sibilant [z] is replaced by the voiced dental non-sibilant fricative [ð], a dentalization in which the grooved sibilant becomes a flat interdental or dental fricative, usually alongside the parallel s > θ. Bashkir and Turkmen did this independently: both have [ð] where Common Turkic and their nearest relatives have z.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Dentalization, Desibilantization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Bashkir",
      "languageFamily": "Kipchak",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Common Turkic *qïz 'girl' (Tatar qız)",
          "to": "ҡыҙ [qɯ̞ð]",
          "note": "Bashkir has [ð] where Turkic generally has z, outside word-initial position (also оҙон 'long', Turkish uzun); Bashkir likewise turned s into [θ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Common Turkic *tuz 'salt' (Tatar тоз)",
          "to": "тоҙ [tʊ̞ð]"
        },
        {
          "from": "Common Turkic *küz 'autumn' (Tatar көз)",
          "to": "көҙ [kʏ̞ð]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Turkmen",
      "languageFamily": "Oghuz",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Common Turkic *qïz 'girl' (Turkish kız)",
          "to": "gyz /ˈɣɯːð/",
          "note": "Unlike the other Oghuz languages, Turkmen realizes Common Turkic /z/ and /s/ as the dental fricatives [ð] and [θ], and has no /z/ or /s/ phonemes."
        },
        {
          "from": "Common Turkic *tuz 'salt' (Turkish tuz)",
          "to": "duz /ˈduːð/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Turkic köz 'eye' (Turkish göz)",
          "to": "göz /ˈɡœð/"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
